A. the witness law   P(|eps| > x) = 1/(x(1+ln x)),  symmetric
   sampler vs survival function, max abs error over the grid: 0.00012
   E|eps| = infinity.  Closed form is exact; the Monte Carlo column tracks it
   only while M stays inside the sample range (max|eps| = 1.62e+05),
   then saturates -- an estimator artefact, marked, not a property of the law:
               M   Monte Carlo   closed form   M in range
           1e+02        2.5469        2.5453   yes
           1e+04        3.2283        3.2255   yes
           1e+08        3.5658        3.9148   no (saturated)
           1e+16        3.5658        4.6070   no (saturated)
           1e+32        3.5658        5.2999   no (saturated)
   Feller's n P(|eps| > n):
       witness   1e+03: 0.1265  1e+06: 0.0675  1e+12: 0.0349  1e+60: 0.0072
        cauchy   1e+03: 0.6366  1e+06: 0.6366  1e+12: 0.6366  1e+60: 0.6366
   -> witness: E|eps| = inf AND n P(|eps|>n) -> 0.   [ok]

B. the claim as stated is false, twice over
   B1  t == 0 satisfies every hypothesis and the average is identically 0.
       Exact; no simulation involved.
   B2  t == v, a fixed unit vector -- bounded, and bounded AWAY from zero.
       The average is |n^{-1} sum eps_k|, and Kolmogorov-Feller gives it
       -> 0 in probability because n P(|eps|>n) -> 0 (part A, closed form),
       the centering vanishing by symmetry.  A theorem, not a simulation.
   B3  Monte Carlo, illustration only, tested against the rate the theory
       predicts.  Spread of the mean is ~ sqrt(2/ln n), so over the reachable
       range the median can fall only by about 1.34x -- the size of
       the MC error itself, which is why monotone decay is not testable here
       and median * sqrt(ln n) is.  It must be flat for the witness law and
       must CLIMB for Cauchy, whose mean is Cauchy(0,1) exactly at every n.
      law                            1000     4000    16000    64000   256000    drop
      witness  1/(x(1+ln x))       0.2232   0.2480   0.1761   0.1704   0.1706   1.31x
        x sqrt(ln n)               0.5865   0.7143   0.5478   0.5668   0.6020   1.03x
      cauchy                       0.9193   1.0386   0.9232   0.9704   0.9291   0.99x
        x sqrt(ln n)               2.4162   2.9911   2.8724   3.2281   3.2788   1.36x
      witness decays at the predicted rate (1.31x observed against 1.34x predicted): yes
      Cauchy pinned at 1, rescaled column climbs: yes
   The proposition predicts 'does not converge' on BOTH rows.  It is right on
   Cauchy and wrong on the witness law, which has E|eps| = infinity all the same.
   -> claim as stated REFUTED (B1, B2 exact); MC consistent [ok]

C. the repair:  limsup_n ||S_n||/n = infinity  a.s.
   C1  Borel-Cantelli series sum_{n<=N} P(|eps| > cn).  It grows like
       ln(1+ln N)/c, so the testable content is that it keeps increasing,
       not that it passes any fixed level:
                   N=1e4     N=1e6     N=1e8   increasing
      c=1          2.963     3.336     3.606   yes
      c=3          0.658     0.772     0.857   yes
   C2  the mechanism on paths:  max_{n in (N/10, N]} |eps_n| / n.  On any
       path where S_n/n converges, eps_n/n -> 0; under E|eps| = inf it does
       not.  Against a finite-mean control the two decay rates separate --
       1/ln N against a power of N:
      law                              10,000    100,000  1,000,000 10,000,000     drop
      witness (E|eps| = inf)           0.5178     0.4012     0.2491     0.3219    1.61x
      pareto-1.5 (E|eps| < inf)        0.1548     0.0781     0.0330     0.0193    8.01x
       exceedance counts #{n<=N : |eps_n| > n}, observed against their exact
       expectation -- an agreement check, since the expectation rises only
       from 2.96 to 3.34 across four decades and cannot be resolved as growth:
                                          2.0        3.0        3.0        2.0   observed
                                        2.963      3.167      3.336      3.480   expected
   Growth is log-log slow by construction, so these numbers ILLUSTRATE the
   theorem; Borel-Cantelli plus z_n/n -> 0 on any convergent path PROVES it.
   -> [ok]

